module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_alias_argument_re_resolved_in_the_importer_scope_loses_its_defining_identity

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode, RungRead, FoundBelowTheLadder, BelowCeiling }
import gunbc.guarantee_rung { MechanicallyPreventable }
import gunbc.rung_climb { ClimbableButUnbuilt }

// AN ALIAS APPLIED ARGUMENT RE-RESOLVED IN THE IMPORTER SCOPE LOSES ITS DEFINING IDENTITY. New error class: a resolve/typing
// refusal (and a misjudged declaration property) caused by nominal-alias expansion re-grounding an alias right-hand side in the
// consumer module scope. It is a compiler defect, not a fork or a capability reconciliation, so it is recorded here rather than in
// gunbc.integration_concern. The arm-114 Measure case is its receipt and one observed instance of the class, not its whole surface.

data an_alias_argument_re_resolved_in_the_importer_scope_loses_its_defining_identity: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_alias_argument_re_resolved_in_the_importer_scope_loses_its_defining_identity" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungRead {
    found_at: FoundBelowTheLadder,
    standing: BelowCeiling {
      current: FoundBelowTheLadder,
      ceiling: MechanicallyPreventable,
      blocker: ClimbableButUnbuilt,
      trigger_capability: "carry each nominal alias its defining module, ground the alias concrete right-hand-side arguments in that defining module while still binding the use-site type parameters in the consumer env, and judge the carried argument node in the equality arm with the judging-scope lookup removed; a name recovered from a path (span.file) is rejected because the name-to-path mapping runs one way only."
    }
  },

  receipts: [
    "THE CLASS. A nominal alias whose right-hand side names cross-module declarations is re-grounded in the CONSUMER module's scope, so the identity resolved at the alias's OWN definition site is discarded and every downstream judgment about an alias-applied argument is made against the consumer's visible-binding set rather than the defining module's. The authority is v1.compiler.infer_resolve::expand_nominal_alias_rhs (v1/compiler/infer/resolve.rs, expand_nominal_alias_rhs_by_name). It takes the CONSUMER env and module_name: it fetches the alias binding via lookup_type_for(env, n) -- which resolves only the ALIAS NAME, because the alias is imported -- and then grounds the binding's stored right-hand side structurally via ground_ungrounded_alias_leaf and resolve_node_bounded against that SAME consumer env. For a parameterized alias that re-grounding is necessary (the use site must bind the alias's own type parameters); for the concrete arguments of a NON-generic alias, whose symbols were resolved against the alias's defining module, re-resolving them as names in the consumer env is the defect: an argument whose owner coproduct the consumer does not import collapses to a bare unbound name, and any declaration-property judgment that should hold (nullary-variant, routed, phantom, conformance) is answered false. The identity existed at the definition site; expansion dropped it.",
    "THE SPECIMEN AND RECEIPT, the arm-114 Measure case. gunbc.compute.compute_seat_ensure::seat_fell_by judges an equality d == granted on Kibibyte = Measure<Memory, Kibi, Nat> (the equality at compute_seat_ensure.dag:167). Memory is the nullary variant Quantity::Memory of type Quantity (std/measure.dag:35); Kibi is the nullary variant Scale::Kibi of type Scale (std/measure.dag:103); Measure<Q, S, M> declares M as the only member (count: M), so Q and S are phantom type parameters. compute_seat_ensure imports std.measure { Kibibyte, kibibyte, measure_sub, MeasureDifference, MeasureSubtrahendExceedsMinuend } but NOT Quantity or Scale. The equality walk v1.compiler.infer::equality_arguments_by_target maps each applied argument through v1.compiler.infer::equality_argument_by_target; Memory is not a by-target declaration, not a kernel type, and not an expandable alias, so it falls to the final arm and v1.compiler.infer::is_unit_variant_phantom_argument (the arm named by the model as admitting it). That arm consults v1.compiler.infer_env::lookup_unit_variant_phantom_type against the JUDGING scope's unit_variant_index, which v1.compiler.infer_env::build_unit_variant_index populates only from coproducts VISIBLE AS BINDINGS in the judging module. A resolve-site instrument read it directly: observed=true but Memory and Kibi are ABSENT from that index (their owner coproducts Quantity and Scale are not imported by the judging module), so the arm returns false and the walk refuses with `equality admission for Measure cannot be judged: a routed member inside a generic application whose shape the equality plan does not model`. The cause text is itself a defect -- Memory is not routed -- but the substance is the class: admission of the alias's phantom argument depends on the judging module's IMPORT SET, not on the argument's own declaration.",
    "THE SURFACE. v1.compiler.infer_resolve::expand_nominal_alias_rhs is UNIVERSAL -- 24 call sites (22 in v1/compiler/infer.rs, 1 in v1/compiler/lookup.rs, 1 in v1/compiler/infer/resolve.rs) -- including the equality-argument arm (infer.rs equality_argument_by_target), the conformance peel, the actual/formal parameter peel, the declared-type peels, the parameter-binding path and the lookup.rs nominal read. Each re-derives alias structure in the judging env, so every import-dependent judgment on an alias-applied argument inherits this defect, not only the Measure case.",
    "SIBLING AUDIT of the consumers of v1.compiler.infer_env::lookup_unit_variant_phantom_type / unit_variant_index / build_unit_variant_index. Only v1.compiler.infer::is_unit_variant_phantom_argument (the arm-114 equality argument) judges an APPLIED argument whose declaring identity is available yet consults the judging-scope index -- that is the same rule. The other three act on an UNRESOLVED bare name, where no identity exists yet and the judging-scope index is therefore the correct authority: v1.compiler.infer_resolve (the resolver resolving a bare name to a unit-variant phantom in the defining scope), v1.compiler.infer_env::type_ref_measure_binding_authority (deciding whether a name is bound), and v1.compiler.emit_rust::type_leaf_ownership_from_visible_unit_arms (deciding ownership among visible unit arms at emit). Those three are kept.",
    "THE EARLIEST UNJUSTIFIED BOUNDARY AND THE REPAIR. The boundary is expand_nominal_alias_rhs_by_name re-resolving the alias's right-hand-side arguments BY NAME in the consumer env instead of CARRYING the identity resolved in the alias's own module. The repair is to carry, not to recover: a rejected alternative recovers the declaring identity from the argument's span.file -- reading a name back from a path -- which inverts the name-to-path mapping the design runs one way only (gunbc.design_argument::single_authority) and patches the symptom link rather than the boundary. B must give each nominal alias binding its defining module, ground the alias's CONCRETE right-hand-side arguments in that defining module while still binding the use-site type parameters in the consumer env (two grounding contexts in one helper), and then judge the carried argument node with the judging-scope lookup removed from the equality arm. It reuses v1.compiler.infer_env::is_unit_variant_node and v1.compiler.infer_env::symbol_index_lookup rather than forking a nullarity test.",
    "SIZE, recorded before writing. B is cross-cutting and sits in the most invariant-guarded region of the resolver. (1) A nominal alias binding (v1.compiler.infer_env::TypeBinding: name, resolved, provenance, alias_rhs) records NO defining module, so one must be added and populated at binding creation. (2) The universal expand helper must split grounding context -- defining module for the concrete arguments, consumer env for the parameters -- inside the resolve.rs invariant block (the masked / source_visible_names / UnlistedImportUse family-closure scaffold; the reviewed 2026-07-06 invariant that grounding recursions descend only into defining-module structure; the 2026-09-09 field-access-alias-peel fix recorded after the census measured definer-tree names charged to importers). (3) The whole v2 corpus must be re-validated. Regressions here surface only in v2-bootstrap and the claim witnesses, because CI evaluates no witnesses. If the climb proves materially larger than recorded here, stop and report the size rather than grind: remote builds are blocked on B.",
    "WHY IT WENT UNNOTICED. compute_seat_ensure.dag reached no executing equality-walk path until gunbc#16781 (13d2196d958, 2026-11-20) added import gunbc.compute.compute_seat_ensure to gunbc.compute.remote_build_serve, pulling seat_fell_by into the serve entry's resolve_entry_graph_inclusive -- the first lane that judged the Kibibyte equality. The latent refusal then refused every serving request; the serve-side seat knock was cut (the revert recorded on gunbc.recurring_failure_mode::a_held_compute_seat_has_no_in_corpus_discharge), which re-opens the frontier that B dissolves -- once an alias-applied argument keeps its defining identity, remote_build_serve may import compute_seat_ensure and still resolve. No CI claim witness covers these files and the job roster is closed, so the gap that let it stay silent is that resolve-time equality refusals in this route reach an executing lane at all; that lane's routing is left to the integration manager.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
